N('boston-carnegie-library-ga','Boston Carnegie Library','Boston','ga','civic','standing',30.79037,-83.79041,'exact','open','walk','','1912–',
  "The mayor wrote to Carnegie on September 9, 1912, for his \"growing and enterprising little city,\" and the town got $6,000. Charles Edward Choate, who also drew the Eatonton and Fitzgerald libraries, designed it with E. D. Ivey of Boston. Wikipedia says it has been a library since December 3, 1912; a local television report says it was built in 1914, and calls Boston the smallest town with a Carnegie library. It was remodelled inside and given a rear addition after 1992, and is a branch of the Thomas County library system.",
  '',
  '','250 South Main Street. A working public library; its hours were not found on its own page.',
  [W('Thomas County Public Library System'),['WTXL: a special library in Boston','https://www.wtxl.com/news/wtxl-road-trip-a-special-library-in-boston/article_9aa085dc-1ac0-11e5-af4a-0b5f6fe9b8ce.html'],LK],
  brick='?',hours='?',conf='mixed'),
N('cordele-carnegie-library-ga','Cordele-Crisp Carnegie Library','Cordele','ga','civic','standing',31.96924,-83.78234,'exact','open','walk','','1903–',
  "Cordele was granted $17,556 in 1903, and Wikipedia says the library was dedicated the next year, making it the second oldest Carnegie library in Georgia. It is one of the few that won a second grant, $7,556, after the head of the Georgia Library Commission asked for it, to enlarge the crowded building. The University of North Florida's record says instead that it opened in 1915, the first library in the state to serve a whole county. Its classical front has a pediment and two columns about an arched door. Still a library in the Lake Blackshear regional system.",
  'A pediment and two columns framing an arched doorway.',
  '','115 East 11th Avenue. A working public library; its hours were not found on its own page.',
  [W('Lake Blackshear Regional Library System'),[UNF+': Carnegie Free Library, Cordele','https://digitalcommons.unf.edu/historical_architecture_main/2797'],LK],
  brick='?',hours='?',conf='mixed'),
N('cuthbert-carnegie-library-ga','Cuthbert Carnegie Library (Randolph County Chamber of Commerce)','Cuthbert','ga','civic','standing',31.77206,-84.79039,'exact','view','walk','','1912–1997',
  "The Woman's Club of Cuthbert won the grant: $7,000 on May 17, 1912, on condition the town paid $700 a year. Vanishing Georgia says it was built for $12,000 on a site the club gave, in the Colonial Revival style, by Peter Dennis, his only Carnegie library in Georgia; the University of North Florida's record says it was dedicated on September 5, 1918. It was the library until 1997, when a new one opened on the square, and is now the Randolph County Chamber of Commerce. In the Cuthbert Historic District.",
  '',
  '','122 Lumpkin Street, at Pearl Street. Chamber of Commerce offices: look from the street.',
  [W('Kinchafoonee Regional Library System'),[VG+': Carnegie Library, 1912, Cuthbert','https://vanishinggeorgia.com/2012/08/30/carnegie-library-1912-cuthbert/'],[UNF+': Cuthbert Carnegie Library','https://digitalcommons.unf.edu/historical_architecture_main/2817'],LK],
  brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('dawson-carnegie-library-ga','Dawson Carnegie Library','Dawson','ga','civic','standing',31.77600,-84.44658,'area','view','walk','','1913–1999',
  "Dawson got $10,000 on March 14, 1913, after stretching its census figures to win a larger building, though some at the Carnegie office thought its subscription library was enough. The library was finished in 1914 and later became the Kinchafoonee Regional Library; it was given up in 1999 for a new building. The state list says it is now an events hall for the Terrell County Historic Preservation Society.",
  '',
  '','334 Main Street NE. An events hall: look from the street.',
  [W('Kinchafoonee Regional Library System'),LK],brick='?'),
N('dublin-carnegie-library-ga','Carnegie Library, Dublin (Dublin Carnegie)','Dublin','ga','civic','standing',32.53864,-82.90830,'exact','view','walk','','1903–1904',
  "Built in 1904 with $10,000 from Carnegie to plans by Thomas H. Morgan and John R. Dillon (Bruce, Morgan and Dillon), with John A. Kelley the builder. It was Dublin's library until a larger one opened in 1964. In the 1970s it faced demolition and the Laurens County Historical Society saved it; it was the society's museum until 2012 (the visitors' bureau) or 2014 (Wikipedia). It is now an events hall and gallery run by downtown Dublin. On the National Register since 1975, and in the Dublin Commercial Historic District.",
  'Its columns outside, and heart pine floors inside.',
  'The visitors\' bureau says to park behind Theatre Dublin or across the street at Bellevue Avenue and Church Street.',
  '311 Academy Avenue, where it meets Bellevue Avenue and Jackson Street. An events hall rented for receptions and art shows: look from the street.',
  [W('Carnegie Library (Dublin, Georgia)'),['Visit Dublin: Dublin Carnegie','https://www.visitdublinga.org/meet/dublin-carnegie/'],['National Register record','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/75000599'],[VG+': Carnegie Library, 1904, Dublin','https://vanishinggeorgia.com/2017/03/26/carnegie-library-1904-dublin/'],LK],
  brick='?',conf='mixed'),
